:: Day 4 EV

On the eleventh day the wardens came for the ferry.

They came at first light in the long boat under the green pennant, four of them, and a fifth Wren did not know who wore no green at all and read from a paper without looking up. The ferry, the ferry house, the crossing and its toll: wardens' property, by right of a book no one in Saltmarrow had ever seen.

Tam listened to the whole of it from the doorway with the brass lantern in her hand.

"Forty years," she said when the man had finished, "and never once a warden on my ferry. I'm not starting today."

The man who had read the paper folded it and put it away, and looked, for the first time, at the lantern.

Wren lit it from the stove, as she had every morning since her seventeenth spring, and carried it down to the jetty past four wardens who did not stop her, and set it in its bracket at the bow.

"Toll," she said to the fifth man, and held out her hand.

:: The toll

That night, the first since leaving Saltmarrow, Wren and Pip slept in the lee of the old mill with the ledger under Pip's head for a pillow.

In the morning the wardens' long boat was tied up at the far jetty, and Captain Marsh stood in the stern with one boot on the gunwale, the way he always stood, dry, with his hat on.

"Toll," he said.

Wren felt for the two copper bits. Pip's hand found hers.

"Two copper bits," Marsh said. "Or the book. Your choice."

She gave him the two copper bits. It was the last money she had. He made his mark in a ledger bound in green cloth and let them pass, and the ferry took them home across water as flat and grey as a plate.

[[Day 4 EV]]

:: Gull Chapel at dusk

They climbed the hill at the end of the day, Wren with the ledger under her arm and Pip a step behind, and the bell began as they reached the top so that the whole tower shook with it.

Brother Ilex let it ring out before he came down. He took the ledger without a word and carried it to the window and turned the last page to the light.

"That is not a warden's hand," he said. "I know every hand on this hill and both banks. That ink is oak gall, and there is one person left who makes it."

I could hear the bell still humming in the beams above us, and I thought of Tam alone at the ferry house with the stove going out.

"Who?" Wren said.

Brother Ilex looked at Pip, and then at Wren, and then at the ring of keys at his belt.

"Ask the Widow Kestle," he said. "And this time, ask her who built the weir."

[[Go down to the ferry->The last ferry]]

:: The last ferry

Wren went down the hill in the dark with Pip's hand in hers. The bell had stopped. Below them the river was a black road with one light on it, and the light was moving.

"That's Tam," Pip said. "That's the lantern."

It was. Old Tam brought the ferry in against the Saltmarrow jetty with the brass lantern burning at the bow, and she did not ask what Brother Ilex had said, and Wren did not tell her. There would be time. Eleven days, less two.

Wren steps aboard and takes the pole from Tam's hands, and Tam lets her, which she has never done before. The ferry swings out into the current. The lantern throws its circle on the water, and the water gives nothing back.

"Across?" Wren said.

"Across," said Tam. "And this time, girl, we knock on the right door."
